Flexure guides, compliance, parasitic motion and range (@mech-flexures: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Elements-of-Mechanical-Design-MIT-2-72.lingua#r_31S9Z88TMF2875BF5KA5825GX7", #source "../sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Gear-geometry-ratio-and-conjugate-motion.lingua#r_2AAYHDQVB8KREPCYQGNFJSC6CP") { r_7V0ZKS4RD134373JWW91K3AS05
**Read:** [Elements of Mechanical Design — MIT 2.72, Spring 2009](../sources/Elements-of-Mechanical-Design-MIT-2-72.lingua#r_31S9Z88TMF2875BF5KA5825GX7), lecture 8; [FUNdaMENTALS of Design — Alexander Slocum](../sources/FUNdaMENTALS-of-Design-Slocum.lingua#r_58Q6VQJ3FXXGXRBJ3DK0AVC3WQ), topic 10's flexural bearings; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), simplified rod theory for the relevant deformation assumptions.

A flexure creates motion by elastic deformation instead of sliding or rolling at a joint. It can remove a particular frictional interface and backlash source, while introducing finite stiffness, limited range, parasitic motions and stress. The constrained directions also have finite compliance; none are perfectly rigid.

For a small-deflection cantilever of length L and bending stiffness EI, a tip transverse force gives δ=FL³/(3EI) and tip rotation FL²/(2EI). A clamped-guided beam with zero end rotation has transverse stiffness 12EI/L³ under its different boundary conditions. Interchanging those formulas changes a prediction by a factor of four.

**Work:** Derive both cases, then compose a parallel-leaf guide and examine its unwanted motion. Compare linear beam theory with a geometrically nonlinear model as displacement grows. Account for manufacturing thickness variation, residual stress, fatigue, buckling and actuator force. State how resolution, repeatability and accuracy would each be measured; a small sensor increment does not establish a small positioning error.

